---
import Snippet from '../components/Snippet.astro';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: 'why-shinro', text: 'Why Shinro' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'how-it-fits', text: 'How it fits' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'where-to-start', text: 'Where to start' },
];

const routeExample = `import { defineHandler } from 'shinro/app';

export const GET = defineHandler((c) => {
  return c.json({ id: c.req.param('id') }, 200);
});`;
---

<Layout
  title="File-based routing for Hono"
  description="Shinro turns route files into a mountable Hono router and an end-to-end typed client."
  {headings}
>
  <h1>File-based routing</h1>

  <p>
    Shinro turns a folder of route modules into a regular Hono router and a
    typed client. Add, move, or remove a route, and both update together without
    a route registry or client types to maintain by hand.
  </p>

  <p>
    One Vite plugin handles discovery, development, RPC types, and builds on
    Node.js or Bun. You keep ownership of the Hono app, middleware order, server
    startup, and graceful shutdown.
  </p>

  <figure class="route-figure">
    <Snippet code={routeExample} title="src/routes/users/$id.ts" />
    <div class="route-preview" aria-label="Shinro route generation flow">
      <div class="route-flow">
        <div class="route-flow-node">Route files</div>
        <span class="route-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        <div class="route-flow-node">Hono router</div>
        <span class="route-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        <div class="route-flow-node">Typed client</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figcaption>
      One route tree drives runtime registration and the client contract.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2 id="why-shinro"><a href="#why-shinro">Why Shinro</a></h2>

  <p>Three properties shape the project:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>One plugin.</strong> <code>shinro()</code> owns route discovery,
      development integration, builds, and RPC generation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your server.</strong> Start Hono with <code>serve()</code> or
      <code>Bun.serve()</code>, and keep lifecycle code in your application.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Readable output.</strong> Generated routes are real TypeScript
      files with one Hono registration per route, ready to inspect or debug.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="how-it-fits"><a href="#how-it-fits">How it fits</a></h2>

  <p>
    <code>routes()</code> returns a Hono sub-router. Mount it wherever it belongs
    in the middleware chain. Hono copies the sub-router schema onto your app, so
    <code>typeof app</code> contains generated and hand-written routes together.
  </p>

  <div class="table-block">
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Shinro owns</th>
          <th>You own</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Route discovery and ordering</td>
          <td>The Hono application</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Directory middleware composition</td>
          <td>Global middleware and error handling</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Generated router and client types</td>
          <td>Server startup and shutdown</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Development and build integration</td>
          <td>Runtime-specific server options</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="where-to-start"><a href="#where-to-start">Where to start</a></h2>

  <p>
    Begin with <a href="/getting-started">installation</a>, then
    <a href="/create-the-app">create and mount the app</a>. Read
    <a href="/file-routes">file routes</a> for the filename rules, and
    <a href="/middleware">directory middleware</a> when a section of the API
    needs shared behavior.
  </p>

  <p>
    Want the complete contract? The
    <a href="/file-conventions">file conventions reference</a> collects every
    mapping and exclusion in one place.
  </p>
</Layout>
